### Step 2: the mapping from try number to file name
The try number of a line row is the number of its cost rows in costs.tsv, and the file name comes
from the output column of that cost row. A call that failed at the transport level costs 0 USD and
writes no file, so it still takes a try number and it still moves the file index forward only when
a later call writes a file. The two numbers therefore differ for 2 rows. The mapping, from the
cost rows of each row and the print files on disk:
- l2-w13-card-throw: line try 4 gives l2-w13-card-throw-m1-line-4.png, and the print file is
  l2-w13-card-throw-m1-line-print-4.png.
- l2-w14-card-windy: line try 4 gives l2-w14-card-windy-m1-line-4.png, and the print file is
  l2-w14-card-windy-m1-line-print-4.png.
- l2-w09-story-page-tom-can-see-the-mop: line try 4 was a network timeout at 0 USD and wrote no
  file, so line try 5 gives l2-w09-story-page-tom-can-see-the-mop-m1-line-4.png, and the print file
  is l2-w09-story-page-tom-can-see-the-mop-m1-line-print-4.png.
- l2-w09-card-rice: line try 4 was a network timeout at 0 USD and wrote no file, so line try 5
  gives l2-w09-card-rice-m1-line-4.png, and the print file is l2-w09-card-rice-m1-line-print-4.png.
- The 4 approval entries name the 4 print files of the list above.

